# Getting Hands-on with Node.js

## Installing
Download from nodejs.org (LTS version is the safe pick), then check:

```bash
node -v
npm -v
```

## The REPL
Run `node` with no arguments to open the REPL (Read, Eval, Print, Loop). Good for quickly testing small bits of code.

```bash
node
> 2 + 2
4
> _ * 10      # _ holds the last result
40
> .help       # list REPL commands
> .exit       # quit (or press Ctrl+C twice)
```

## Running a file
```js
// app.js
console.log("Hello from Node");
```
```bash
node app.js
```

## Global objects
In the browser the global object is `window`. In Node.js it's `global`.

- `global` is Node-specific.
- `globalThis` is the standard, works everywhere (Node and browser). Prefer this.
- `setTimeout()` and `setInterval()` are available globally in Node too.

```js
console.log(globalThis === global); // true in Node

setTimeout(() => console.log("runs once after 1s"), 1000);

const id = setInterval(() => console.log("runs every 1s"), 1000);
clearInterval(id); // stop it
```

Note: a `var` declared at the top of a file does **not** become a property of `global`, because each file is its own module scope (more in the modules notes).